drag.payload.section.overview.body:
`DragPayload` encapsulates data transferred between the drag initiator and candidate drop zones.

### Core Architecture

- **`id`**: Unique identifier for tracking the dragged instance or item.
- **`type`**: String MIME or semantic type (e.g. `item/inventory`, `dock/tab`). Supports wildcard prefix matching such as `item/*`.
- **`preview`**: Brief text representation displayed in default ghosts and tooltips.
- **`data`**: Optional attached in-memory Java object (`Object`), accessible via `data(Class<T>)`.

```java
DragPayload payload = DragPayload.of("item-42", "inventory/slot", "Diamond Sword", swordItem);
if (payload.matchesType("inventory/*")) {
    Sword item = payload.data(Sword.class);
}
```

drag.ghost.section.overview.body:
`DragGhost` renders visual feedback underneath the pointer on the top-level overlay during drag gestures.

### Built-in Factories

| Method | Behavior |
| --- | --- |
| `DragGhost.widget(widget, opacity)` | Renders a clone of the source widget with custom opacity (e.g. 0.8f). |
| `DragGhost.text()` | Renders a styled badge containing `session.payload().preview()`. |

### Custom Rendering

Implement `render(RenderContext context, DragSession session)` to render arbitrary shapes, item icons, or multi-item stacks:

```java
DragGhost custom = (context, session) -> {
    context.rect(session.pointerX() + 8, session.pointerY() + 8, 32, 32, Paint.fill(ColorView.CYAN));
};
```

drag.over.event.section.overview.body:
`DragOverEvent` is dispatched continuously as the pointer hovers over candidate drop widgets.

### Target Validation

Drop zones inspect `payload()` and confirm acceptance by calling `event.accept(this)`:

```java
widget.addListener(DragOverEvent.class, event -> {
    if (event.payload().matchesType("inventory/*")) {
        event.accept(widget);
    }
});
```

When accepted, the widget becomes `session.dropTarget()`, enabling visual hover styling and indicator lines.

drag.drop.event.section.overview.body:
`DragDropEvent` is delivered to the accepted drop target when the user releases the pointer.

### Handling Drops

```java
widget.addListener(DragDropEvent.class, event -> {
    MyData item = event.payload().data(MyData.class);
    insertItem(item);
});
```

Delivering `DragDropEvent` successfully marks the drag session outcome as `DragOutcome.DROPPED`.
